C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410 Fall 2026
      Problem 7.1 from 
       
      Maximize profits from irrigation by optimizing the development and use of potential dams and water pump.
       
      Izzy Hinton
      isabel.hinton@usu.edu
      October 19, 2026
  11   
  12  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  13  SETS
  14  src irrigation water sources /hd "high dam", ld "low dam", rd "river diversion"/
  15  time season that irrigation occurs in /w "winter", s "spring"/;
  16   
  17  * 2. DEFINE input data
  18  PARAMETERS
  19     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  20           /hd 10000,
  21            ld 6000,
  22            rd 8000/
  23     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  24           /hd 0,
  25            ld 0,
  26            rd 20/
  27    WaterDemand(time) volume of water required for an acre of crop (ac-ft per acre)
  28          /w 1,
  29           s 3/
  30  * river diversion capacity was given in ac-ft/day multiplying by 365/2 gives the semi-annual capacity
  31      Capacity(src) maximum volume of water that can be extraced from a source (ac-ft)
  32          /hd 700,
  33          ld 300,
  34          rd 401.5/
  35      Inflow(time) volume of water flowing into the reservoir each season (ac-ft)
  36          /w 600,
  37           s 200/
  38      Revenue rate of revenue produced from each irrigated acre ($ per acre) /300/
  39  *GroundWater is 2 ac-ft/day multiplied by 365/2 for the gw for 1 time step
  40      GroundWater volume of water that is added to the river flow through groundwater (ac-ft) /365/;
  41   
  42  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  43  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or develop project from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  44            X(src,time) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  45            TPROFIT total profit (revenue from irrigation minus cost to irrigate) ($);
  46   
  47  BINARY VARIABLES I;
  48   
  49  * Non-negativity constraints
  50  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  51   
  52  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  53  EQUATIONS
  54     TotalRev             Total revenue ($) and objective function value
  55     OneDam              Only one dam can be built
  56     MassBalanceRiver    Mass Balance for river diversion
  57     MassBalanceRes       Mass Balance for reservoir diversion
  58     CapacityConstraint  The water from each source must be less than its' maximum capacity;
  59   
  60  TotalRev..                 TPROFIT =E= SUM((src,time), Revenue*WaterDemand(time)*X(src,time))-Sum(src, CapCost(src)*I(src)) - SUM((src,time), OpCost(src)*X(src,time));
  61  OneDam..                         I("hd") + I("ld") =L= 1;
  62  MassBalanceRiver(time)..         X("rd", time) =L= Inflow(time) - X("hd",time) - X("ld", time) + GroundWater;
  63  MassBalanceRes(time)..           X("hd",time) + X("ld",time) =L= Inflow(time);
  64  CapacityConstraint(src,time)..    X(src,time) =L= Capacity(src)*I(src);
  65   
  66  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  67  MODEL IrrigationSources /ALL/;
  68   
  69  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  70  SOLVE IrrigationSources USING MIP MAXIMIZING TPROFIT;
  71   
  72   
  73   
  74   


